// Default methods on an interface.
//
// An interface is a contract. `Bind <type> to <name> unto <interface>` gives that contract a body
// as well as a signature, so every conforming type gets the method without writing it. A type that
// wants something different writes its own, and its own wins.
//
// The trick worth noticing: a default is written ONCE against the contract, but each conformer gets
// its own specialised copy. `one` inside a default is the conforming object — a shelter, a bird —
// never the interface, because no value is ever of interface type. That is what lets a default read
// fields the interface never mentioned.

Define creature as an interface for {
    The text function name,
    The number function legs,
    The text function describe,
    The text function greeting
}.

// Two defaults. `describe` is written against the contract and calls back into it, so it works for
// anything that conforms. Note `cast name on one` rather than `one's name` — the possessive would
// hand back the method itself instead of calling it.
Bind text to describe unto creature:
    Define legs-text as (cast legs on one) converted to text.
    Return "{cast name on one} walks on {legs-text} legs".
Done.

Bind text to greeting unto creature:
    Return "...".
Done.

// A hare takes nothing but its own name, and takes the defaults as they are.
Define object hare with (the text called) and creature.

Bind text to name unto hare:
    Return one's called.
Done.

Bind number to legs unto hare:
    Return 4.
Done.

// A magpie overrides the greeting, and inherits `describe` unchanged.
Define object magpie with (the text called, the number counted) and creature.

Bind text to name unto magpie:
    Return one's called.
Done.

Bind number to legs unto magpie:
    Return 2.
Done.

Bind text to greeting unto magpie:
    Return "chatter x{one's counted converted to text}".
Done.

// A beetle overrides `describe` instead, and reaches a field no other conformer has.
Define object beetle with (the text called, the text shell) and creature.

Bind text to name unto beetle:
    Return one's called.
Done.

Bind number to legs unto beetle:
    Return 6.
Done.

Bind text to describe unto beetle:
    Return "{one's called} is {one's shell} and keeps its own counsel".
Done.

// The parameter is typed by the interface, so every call site passes a concrete conformer and each
// one compiles to its own specialisation. No vtable, no type tag.
Bind void to introduce, given (the creature who):
    State cast describe on who.
    State "  it says: {cast greeting on who}".
Done.

Define hopper as a new hare { the called "Hopper" }.
Define pica as a new magpie { the called "Pica", the counted 3 }.
Define scarab as a new beetle { the called "Scarab", the shell "green" }.

State "Three creatures, one contract:".
State "".
Cast introduce on hopper.
Cast introduce on pica.
Cast introduce on scarab.

State "".
State "Which came from a default:".
State "  hare    describe: inherited   greeting: inherited".
State "  magpie  describe: inherited   greeting: its own".
State "  beetle  describe: its own     greeting: inherited".
